== UI MVVM boundaries

`search.mjs` is the model for Photon requests and result validation. It enforces the 10-second timeout and accepts cancellation. `search-view-model.mjs` exposes observable query, loading, result, status and panel state, plus search, selection and dismissal commands. A request generation and cancellation prevent a dismissed or superseded response from replacing newer state. `search-view.mjs` binds controls to those commands, renders the state and issues the map camera action on selection. `app.js` composes these pieces with the map. The view-model has no DOM, MapLibre or network dependency; its search function is injected and tested independently.

The project dropdown retains original catalog names and stable IDs. `project-model.mjs` validates site data, derives eligibility and reads or saves membership; `project-view-model.mjs` owns selection, shared loading, save state and coverage presentation; `project-view.mjs` renders full-page Project Overview and MapLibre effects. Overview contains project context and site lists above the lower task-management table, while the right-side popup offers Tasks, Virtual UEs and Network with one close button. `context-panel.mjs` switches these views. Overview makes the obscured map, camera controls and search inert; Back to map or Escape restores them and returns focus to navigation. On phones, opening Overview closes the navigation drawer. Network resolves to the region's adapter and disabled layers show an explicit empty state.

`task-model.mjs` provides timed GET/PUT access to project task lists and POST access to the local road-routing endpoint. Task PUT returns accepted database records with canonical SQL metrics, and the view-model publishes those records only after success. `task-data.mjs` contains pure task validation, atomic GeoJSON import and route-feature derivation. `task-view-model.mjs` holds separate per-project task caches, draft, selection, loading, saving, errors and unfinished waypoint drawing. Project switches retain drafts in memory; late load responses update only their own project cache. A failed initial load cannot overwrite saved tasks because editing stays disabled until a successful retry. Save publishes a new saved list only after server acceptance; failure keeps the draft. Within a project, Save or Discard is required before switching tasks. `task-view.mjs` binds forms, imports, map clicks, route layers and endpoint markers. Drawing consumes map clicks before network/building selection, and Escape cancels the unfinished route. Start and End coordinates are derived from the same first/last vertices used by the route line, never persisted as independent positions. View-model dependency tests enforce separation from browser and network APIs.

The local task data shape is an array of `{id, name, routes, driveSpeedKmh?}`; each route has `{id, name, coordinates}`. Task IDs are project-unique; route IDs are task-unique. The server independently validates identifiers, nonempty names, finite WGS84 positions, two distinct points, and limits of 100 tasks, 50 routes per task, 5000 vertices per route and 10000 project vertices. GeoJSON import accepts only LineString/MultiLineString, including Features and FeatureCollections; it drops optional altitude and rejects unsupported or invalid input atomically. Legacy or unmatched paths remain original geometry, while a successful fit replaces coordinates with OSRM driving geometry. Optional `roadFit` (fitted/unmatched), `waypoints` (2-100 valid guide points) and `fitMessage` (at most 240 characters) preserve fitting state and refit guidance. Both client and server validate these fields; old API task shapes remain valid; persistence upgrades migrate their data transactionally. Unsaved drafts are page-session state, not SQLite records.


`speedCases` is the editable task array of `{id, name, speedKmh}` definitions. The client accepts comma-separated speeds, validates at most 20 unique finite values from 0.1 to 200 km/h, retains IDs for unchanged cases and maintains raw input plus validation state separately from saved definitions. Empty input means no speed assumption. `driveSpeedKmh` remains a compatibility alias for the first case; legacy scalar updates can update that case, and a scalar-only record becomes one case. New clients keep the alias synchronized. Case IDs are task-scoped and default labels are generated.

`taskDriveMetrics` computes unsaved draft estimates using the existing 6371000 m haversine radius. Saved task metrics instead come from SQLite views and are supplied as read-only `driveMetrics` by the API. Each case exposes its own task duration and route durations while sharing route geometry and endpoints. A range is shown when multiple cases exist; individual results are available in expanded details. Missing speed or routes yields unknown duration. Traffic, stops, elevation and transfers are excluded; unfitted geometry is provisional.

`task-table-view.mjs` binds the lower Overview table to the same view-model as the map editor. Stable row elements retain editing focus while values and computed results update. One active draft prevents conflicting task selection. Form-associated inputs use native validation; Save consumes accepted database data, Cancel discards the draft, and Delete confirms before persistence. A route count expands shared-route and per-case detail rows. Map editing carries the current draft rather than copying it. Phone tables provide keyboard-accessible horizontal scrolling and retain task identification. `task-format.mjs` shares coordinate, distance and duration formatting with `task-view.mjs`.

`task_store.py` owns validation, schema initialization, migration and relational reads/writes. `drive_tasks` stores task identity, order, name and extension attributes; `drive_routes` stores route identity/order and fit metadata; `drive_route_points` stores each longitude/latitude with path/guide kind and ordinal; `drive_speed_cases` stores case ID/order/name and speed. Composite foreign keys cascade child deletion, and each application connection enables foreign keys. Whole-project saves validate before deletion and insert the replacement graph in one transaction. Unknown task/route extension attributes remain JSON metadata, while primary fields and geometry are typed rows.

`drive_route_details` uses SQL window lag and haversine math over path points only. `drive_task_details` sums route distances and derives ordered endpoints. `drive_task_speeds` exposes the first case as the compatibility speed. `drive_speed_case_details` calculates each task-case duration, and `drive_route_case_details` calculates each route-case duration. These are native SQLite views, requiring window and math functions rather than application-specific UDFs. They reflect database changes without browser recalculation or stale copied GPS/duration columns. `read_tasks` assembles ordered API geometry and guidance from point rows and attaches SQL metrics; submitted derived metrics are ignored.

A savepoint wraps task-store initialization. Legacy JSON lists migrate once and their table is renamed `project_tasks_legacy`; the archive is not reapplied after later edits or deletions. Original IDs, ordering, coordinates, fit metadata and extension attributes are retained. A prior relational scalar-speed column is backfilled into case rows before removal. Mixed legacy/new stores or invalid source records fail closed and roll back. The API paths and task-array transport remain compatible. Multi-user conflict detection is not implemented, so concurrent whole-project saves retain last-writer behavior.

`roadGuidePoints` selects at most 100 ordered guide points by repeatedly retaining the largest perpendicular bend in the remaining path sections; first and last points are always retained. It runs only for paths above the guide-point limit. `task-view-model.mjs` fits newly drawn/imported routes automatically and exposes Fit to roads for existing paths. Fitting runs sequentially with visible route progress. An AbortController and current-operation identity reject canceled or superseded results; project changes and discard cancel fitting. Failed requests keep original geometry and mark it unmatched; failed refits retain the previous fitted geometry. A candidate is checked against all current project tasks before use, including the 10000-vertex project limit. Save is disabled while fitting is pending.

`road_routing.py` sends bounded GET requests to the configured OSRM driving route endpoint with full GeoJSON geometry, no alternatives and a 150 m snap radius at each guide point. The local browser sends only numeric coordinates to a fixed local POST endpoint; it cannot choose an upstream URL. The server identifies the application in User-Agent, allows one upstream operation at a time, spaces requests by at least one second and caches 32 successful coordinate lists in memory. Busy, unreachable, malformed, disconnected or over-limit results produce a 503 response rather than a false fitted path. Upstream timeout is 8 seconds; browser timeout is 12 seconds. Default base URL is `https://routing.openstreetmap.de/routed-car`; `NDT_ROUTING_URL` permits an operator-controlled OSRM endpoint. Public-service coordinate disclosure, attribution and limitations are shown in the route editor; see #link("https://routing.openstreetmap.de/about.html")[the service policy] and #link("https://project-osrm.org/docs/v26.6.1/http")[the OSRM route contract]. Fitting does not write SQLite until Save task. Routing data can differ from map tiles and does not verify current physical road access.

For New York reservations, `reservations-model.mjs` loads and validates the regional dataset, `reservations-view-model.mjs` holds visibility, query, selection and load state and calculates the nearest matches, and `nyc-reservations.mjs` binds the controls and map. Closing the panel does not change the layer switch; opening its navigation section or selecting a marker shows it again. Each view-model can be exercised with an injected model.

For Toronto network exploration, `network-model.mjs` loads and validates the TELUS and Bell collections using `network-data.mjs`. `network-view-model.mjs` holds loading, filters, selection, query and planning inputs and derives the visible list, markers, sectors and fan status from the radio model. `network-view.mjs` binds the panel and map, including retry. Closing the panel leaves layer switches, selected site and bearing, and rendered map features unchanged; selecting a site or opening the Network section shows it again. The view-model has no DOM, MapLibre, Three.js or network dependency.

`region-model.mjs` loads the region catalog with an eight-second abort timeout, `region-view-model.mjs` resolves the requested region and navigation URL, and `region-view.mjs` populates the labeled native REGION dropdown, selects the active region and binds changes to navigation. The dropdown is disabled until the catalog loads. The application composes these modules before creating the map. A bootstrap handler in `index.html` exposes a reload retry if the map library or a module fails to load, and a 35-second fallback covers a startup that never reaches the normal map or region error handling. The map view-model separately reports its 20-second startup timeout; a late successful map load can still recover.

`navigation-view-model.mjs` holds observable drawer state without browser dependencies. `navigation-view.mjs` binds the menu, responsive breakpoint, dismissal and focus behavior. At 700 pixels or less the drawer closes after choosing a project. The task editor is rendered outside navigation in a height-limited, scrollable right-side panel. The active-project shortcut opens and focuses the separate full-page Project Overview. Selected-boundary fitting accounts for the visible panel and camera controls. The Region overview navigation item is removed; the camera Home button retains the catalog-based overview action.

`map-controls-view-model.mjs` owns layer settings, camera labels and command values, terrain status and warning timing, and shadow presentation derived from `shadows.mjs`. `map-controls-view.mjs` translates control events and MapLibre observations into view-model updates and applies the resulting display and map commands. Terrain tile errors remain visible until terrain is disabled; delayed elevation can recover after loading. The timer and shadow calculation can be injected in tests.

`map-scene-view-model.mjs` manages the startup timeout and selected basemap feature; `map-scene-view.mjs` creates MapLibre sources and Three.js layers, binds map events and renders the loading and selection states. The application entry point composes these parts. A boundary test checks that view-model dependency graphs do not reach browser, network or renderer APIs. No change to the limits of the underlying map or radio data is implied.

= Region configuration and startup

The server binds before it initializes the database. Without `--port`, it tries 5179 through 5189 and advances only for an address-in-use error. An explicit port is exact. The default host is `127.0.0.1`. After optional migration, initialization reads `ndt/regions.json`, validates the presence of required region files, creates tables, updates region metadata and refreshes changed asset blobs.

Each region record contains an ID, display name, map center, zoom, pitch, project shortcuts, network adapter identifier, data root, roof file and network files. Toronto uses the `ised` adapter and assets under `ndt/dist/`; New York uses `nyc-poles` and assets under `ndt/data/new-york/`. Tree and street-detail directories are discovered recursively. The asset fingerprint hashes relative paths, file sizes and modification times, rather than file contents. If it changes, that region's asset blobs and individual network records are replaced together at startup. An invalid import rolls back the refresh. Existing project membership is not deleted by that refresh.

The browser retrieves `/api/regions`, selects the `region` URL parameter or defaults to Toronto, and builds the controls. A region switch assigns a new URL and reloads the page. On map load, the scene view creates the sources and layers, starts the regional network adapter, and, for Toronto, loads all project clusters. The geographic style and terrain remain external runtime dependencies.

= Persistence and service contract

== SQLite schema

#table(columns: (1fr, 2fr, 1.4fr),
  table.header([*Table*], [*Fields and key*], [*Purpose*]),
  [`regions`], [`id` primary key; `metadata` JSON; `fingerprint` text], [Catalog and asset-refresh marker.],
  [`region_assets`], [`region_id`, `path`, `content` blob; composite primary key], [Region-specific imported files.],
  [`network_datasets`], [`region_id`, `path`, collection `metadata` JSON], [Network collection metadata; composite key.],
  [`network_sites`], [`region_id`, `dataset_path`, `site_id`, `ordinal`, `feature` JSON; generated `longitude` and `latitude`], [One complete source feature per site or reservation; region, dataset and site ID form the key.],
  [`drive_tasks`], [Region/project/task ID, ordinal, name and extension attributes], [One row per task; project-scoped ordering and identity.],
  [`drive_routes`], [Task key, route ID/order/name and fit metadata], [One row per route; cascades from its task.],
  [`drive_route_points`], [Route key, path/guide kind, ordinal, longitude and latitude], [Ordered geometry and guidance with coordinate bounds.],
  [`drive_speed_cases`], [Task key, case ID/order/name and speed in km/h], [Unique test speeds; all cases share route geometry.],
  [`drive_*_details`], [Native SQL views for tasks, routes and their speed cases], [Queryable distance, duration and GPS values.],
  [`project_tasks_legacy`], [Original region/project JSON payloads, when upgraded], [Archived migration input; not a live task store.],
  [`project_use_cases`], [`region_id`, `project_id`, checked `use_case`; composite primary key], [One optional purpose category per configured project; independent of site/task data.],
  [`virtual_ues`], [`region_id`, `project_id`, `ue_id`, name, longitude and latitude; composite primary key], [Individual project-scoped virtual UE locations.],
  [`project_sites`], [`region_id`, `project_id`, `site_ids` JSON; composite primary key], [Explicit project membership overrides.],
)

Network responses are assembled from the individual records in their original order; the original network blob remains as an imported snapshot. On upgrade, missing network tables are populated from the existing SQLite asset blobs when the region fingerprint is unchanged; otherwise the region is refreshed from source. Site coordinates are generated from the feature JSON and indexed with region and dataset. Startup validates site IDs and point coordinates. With an unchanged fingerprint, restart preserves the site records; a changed region asset triggers reimport of that region. The viewer has no site-details editing interface or audit history.

The default path is `regions.sqlite3` in the repository root. If that file does not exist and the legacy `ndt/var/regions.sqlite3` does, `migrate_database` uses SQLite's backup API into a temporary file and publishes it with a hard link. It does not overwrite an existing destination and retains the old file. An explicitly supplied `--db` path bypasses this default-path migration. Operators must stop older server instances before migration: an older process can continue writing its old file after the copy. Project edits are not reproducible from asset source files, so the SQLite database needs backup if edits must be retained.

